<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Assyrian Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Assyria, Assyrians, Assyrian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Assyrian Empire was a Mesopotamian state centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>. Rising as a great power in the fourteenth century BCE and reaching its height in the Neo-Assyrian period, it conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> and even <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> using a highly organised army and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">mass deportations</a>. It collapsed between 612 and 609 BCE under the attacks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire.jpg" alt="Assyrian Empire" width="320" height="199" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Austen Henry Layard (1817–1894) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashur_symbol_Nimrud.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Third Dynasty of Ur (c. 2112–2004 BCE), the Old Babylonian kingdom, the <b>Assyrian Empire</b> and ultimately the Achaemenid Empire all drew on administrative, ideological and cultural precedents set in the Akkadian period, and later Mesopotamian kings revered Sargon and Naram-Sin as models of kingship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The rapid unravelling of this economic empire after Hammurabi's death revealed its fragility: the breakaway of the Sealand Dynasty in the south and the loss of influence in <b>Assyria</b> within a generation removed the revenues that had sustained the imperial administration.</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From the ninth century BCE it paid tribute to the <b>Assyrian Empire</b>: Ashurnasirpal II received its gifts on his march to the Mediterranean, and later kings of Byblos appear in the tribute lists of Tiglath-Pileser III and Sennacherib.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Where <b>Assyria</b> had been an empire of continuous expansion, Babylon under Nebuchadnezzar operated mainly through annual campaigns in the west, siege warfare and the enforcement of vassalage, rather than through a dense network of garrisoned provinces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Assyria</b> fell in 612 BCE the island passed under Babylonian control, and by the reign of Nebuchadnezzar II or his successors it was ruled by a Babylonian governor.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dilmun">Dilmun · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Amarna Letters, the diplomatic archive recovered from Akhenaten's capital, reveal a sophisticated system of international correspondence conducted in Akkadian cuneiform, through which Egypt managed its relationships with Babylon, Mitanni, <b>Assyria</b>, the Hittites, and Alashiya (Cyprus).</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
